[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-30", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 30 — the SUT transacts: INS2/INS8 in the mix, every verifiable write visible; 1960 / 3791 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-30.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 30 - the SUT transacts: two inserts in the mix, every verifiable write visible to its read\n\n> Seed: M2 (updates), taken while ADR-2609121100 waits for a decision.\n> Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung SF1 subset; origin :cross-host,\n> path :lan -- SUT on levi, driver on judah (a new column: simeon lost\n> its residency to an 8 GB fleet python process that my full-SF1 run\n> had pushed into swap; reported, not touched).\n\n## Generate\n\n  - SUT: the db value in an atom; POST /ldbc/update swaps it with\n    eng/transact -- one writer, and every read takes the value it finds\n    (a snapshot per read, no lock on the read path). INS2 (a like: the\n    reified 3 datoms) and INS8 (a friendship: knows both ways + the\n    reified date both ways) served; every other insert a 400.\n  - Driver: --updates takes the LDBC update stream; INS2/INS8 go into\n    the round-robin mix at the reads' rate (the certified driver\n    interleaves by simulation time -- a stated difference). After each\n    sweep, read-after-write: the last 20 inserts per run must be visible\n    to the covering read (INS8 -> IS3, INS2 -> IC07 on the post's\n    creator); an acknowledged insert that is not visible refuses the run.\n\n  Prediction: a write costs about a point read served; the 13-op mix\n  lands near the 11-op read-only mix; read-after-write holds.\n\n## Rank\n\n  Three refusals before the row, each a real thing the check found:\n    1. INS2 on a post outside the subset: no creator -> nothing to read.\n    2. INS8 to a person INS1 would have created: IS3 needs a name.\n    3. \"known\" measured as \"has any datom\" said true for those persons\n       -- an earlier INS8 had asserted knows onto them. Known = has a\n       person/firstName.\n  Each is now reported by the SUT (:creator, :p2-known) and counted as\n  UNVERIFIABLE by the driver -- printed, never visible.\n\n    slots  rate  on-time%  thru/s   ins2  ins8  is3   ic09   read-after-write\n      8    2048  100.00   1960.4    1.9   1.9   7.8   7.4    43 checked, 0 not visible, 77 unverifiable\n      8    2560   47.41   2146.4\n     32    4096   95.50   3790.8    3.5   3.5  10.0   9.6    29 checked, 0 not visible, 91 unverifiable\n\n  The SUT applied tens of thousands of inserts across the sweeps and its\n  heap stayed at 1.9-2.3 GB of 6.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Held on all three. A write is a point read's price here because the\n    datom plane's write is a path copy in three persistent maps, and the\n    single writer is an atom swap.\n  - The unverifiable count is most of the sample: the stream's likes land\n    on posts the subset does not hold, and its friendships involve\n    persons INS1 makes. That is the honest edge of a subset rung with\n    updates -- the writes are real, the reads that would prove them are\n    not there. Full SF1 with INS1 served would make nearly all of them\n    verifiable (and iteration 26 says full SF1 needs the ADR or a host).\n  - Not here: dependency tracking, the interleave by simulation time,\n    and the ACID suite. M2 is 2/8 with a visibility check, not \"updates\".\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict: BASELINE for the mix-with-writes on the levi column (1960 at\n8 slots, 3791 at 32). M1 11/29 with reference; M2 2/8 with\nread-after-write.\n\nSeeds for iteration 31, in order:\n\n  - S21 (more inserts): INS1 (person), INS6/INS7 (post/comment), INS3\n    (comment like) -- each makes more of the stream verifiable and each\n    adds a served read's dependency (IS1/IS4/IS7 on the new entities).\n  - S22 (interleave): schedule inserts by the stream's simulation time\n    under the same TCR as the reads, with the stream's dependency time\n    honoured -- the driver's real shape.\n  - On decision: ADR-2609121100, then SF1 proper on levi.\n"}]
